<h2>Withdrawal: abandoning the challenge, keeping the consequences</h2>
<p>An appellant is master of his appeal and may abandon it, wholly or against some respondents only. But the arithmetic differs fundamentally from withdrawing a suit. A plaintiff withdrawing a suit (without liberty) loses the claim; an appellant withdrawing an appeal loses the challenge — and thereby hands the respondent a final, confirmed decree. Three consequences follow: the decree becomes executable without the cloud of the appeal; findings in it bind the parties as res judicata; and any interim stay or deposit arrangement in the appeal dissolves, with the respondent entitled to the fruits of the decree, including amounts deposited.</p>

<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><h3>Partial withdrawal</h3><p>An appeal can be withdrawn against some respondents or confined to some reliefs; what is given up attains finality, what is pressed continues. Cross-objections by the respondent survive the appeal&rsquo;s withdrawal and can still be heard.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Costs and conditions</h3><p>The appellate court may impose costs on withdrawal, and where the appeal was used to hold off execution, respondents legitimately press for compensatory costs or interest for the period of the stay.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Strategic timing</h3><p>Withdrawal before the appeal is heard looks very different from withdrawal after adverse observations in court. The earlier the decision, the cleaner the exit — and the better the negotiating position in any parallel settlement.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Compromise in appeal: a new decree replaces the old</h2>
<p>Settlement at the appellate stage is common — the trial judgment has clarified the risks, and both sides can now price them. The machinery is Order XXIII Rule 3: a lawful agreement, in writing, signed by the parties, which the court verifies before decreeing. Done properly, the compromise decree supersedes the decree under appeal to the extent of its terms; done loosely, it plants the seeds of the next decade&rsquo;s litigation.</p>

<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Reduce every term to writing</strong> — payment schedules, possession dates, mutation steps, withdrawal of connected cases — and have the parties themselves sign, not only counsel.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Verify authority</strong> — settlements signed by attorneys, karta or company officers need the authority documents on record; this is the ground most often attacked later.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Have the court record satisfaction</strong> — a decree should recite that the court examined the parties and found the compromise lawful and voluntary.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Map connected proceedings</strong> — execution petitions, contempt applications, criminal complaints between the parties: say expressly what happens to each.</div>
</div>

<h2>The bar that makes the hearing decisive</h2>
<p>Order XXIII Rule 3A bars a separate suit to set aside a compromise decree, and consent decrees are not appealable. The only door left is an application to the recording court alleging that there was in law no lawful agreement — fraud, coercion, forgery, want of authority. Courts guard that door narrowly. For parties, the message is that the compromise hearing is not a formality: objections to voluntariness or authority must be raised then, on the record, because the law is designed to make that moment final.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>A settlement reached in mediation attached to the appellate court follows the same route — reduced to writing, signed, and decreed under Rule 3. And where the dispute is matrimonial or quasi-criminal proceedings travel with the civil fight, the compromise should deal with them expressly; a decree that settles the property but forgets the pending complaints settles nothing.</p>
</div>
